    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#norodom-of-cambodia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norodom-of-cambodia.jpg" alt="Norodom of Cambodia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#norodom-of-cambodia">Norodom of Cambodia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Norodom,_the_King_of_Cambodia_Wellcome_V0037202.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina">French Indochina</a> was assembled from conquests made over three decades. A Franco-Spanish expedition attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#da-nang">Da Nang</a> in 1858, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-saigon-1862">Treaty of Saigon</a> of 1862 and the annexation of 1867 turned southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a> into the colony of French Cochinchina. In 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norodom-of-cambodia">King Norodom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a> accepted a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">protectorate</a> to escape pressure from Siam and Vietnam. After the deaths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-garnier">Francis Garnier</a> in 1873 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henri-riviere">Henri Rivière</a> in 1883 in Tonkin, the Harmand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-hue-1884">Patenôtre</a> treaties of 1883–1884 made central and northern Vietnam the protectorates of Annam and Tonkin under the Nguyen emperors, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> of 1884–1885 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> to renounce its suzerainty. By a decree of 17 October 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> joined Cochinchina, Annam, Tonkin and Cambodia in the Indochinese Union under a governor-general. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#franco-siamese-war">Franco-Siamese crisis of 1893</a> added the Lao lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mekong-river">Mekong</a>, which were organised as the protectorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a> and attached to the Union in 1899, and China leased the territory of Guangzhouwan to France in 1898. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paul-doumer">Paul Doumer</a>, governor-general from 1897 to 1902, gave the federation its lasting shape: he created a general budget and federal services, subordinated the Vietnamese court, and moved the seat of government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanoi">Hanoi</a> in 1902.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh City" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ho_Chi_Minh_City,_City_Hall,_2020-01_CN-03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steffen Schmitz (more photos), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina">Indochina</a>’s economy was built on exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rubber">rubber</a> and minerals. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mekong-river">Mekong</a> delta, French canal-digging opened vast new rice lands, much of it acquired by French and Vietnamese landlords and farmed by indebted tenants, and by the 1930s Indochina was among the world’s leading rice exporters, shipping through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> and the Chinese merchant town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cholon">Cholon</a>. Rubber plantations in the red-earth lands of Cochinchina and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a>, owned by companies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#michelin">Michelin</a>, recruited labourers from Tonkin and Annam under harsh contracts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coal">coal</a> mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hon-gai">Hon Gai</a> in Tonkin supplied the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bank-of-indochina">Bank of Indochina</a> issued the currency and financed trade. The federal budget relied on indirect taxes and the state monopolies on opium, salt and alcohol, which forced villages to buy set quotas of official alcohol and became deeply resented, while head and land taxes fell heavily on peasants. Society remained mainly rural and Buddhist, with Confucian traditions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a> and Cambodia, and growing Catholic and new religious communities, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caodaism">Cao Dai</a>, founded in 1926. A Western-educated Vietnamese elite emerged, the romanised <em>quoc ngu</em> script spread through schools and newspapers, and the Confucian examinations were abolished in 1915 in Tonkin and in 1919 in Annam. European settlers remained few, numbering in the tens of thousands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ko-chang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ko-chang.jpg" alt="Battle of Ko Chang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ko-chang">Battle of Ko Chang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Koh_Chang_17_january_1941_(English_version).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Xiengyod (original work by user:historicair), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>French rule rested on a colonial army that combined French regulars, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-foreign-legion">Foreign Legion</a> and North African and Senegalese units with large numbers of Indochinese riflemen (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a></em>) recruited locally. In its early years it fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#can-vuong">Can Vuong</a> resistance and armed bands in the Tonkin uplands, notably the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hoang-hoa-tham">De Tham</a> in Yen The, who held out until 1913. Some 90,000 to 100,000 Indochinese soldiers and labourers were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a>. Within the colony the army and the Sûreté, the political police, crushed uprisings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yen-bai-mutiny">Yen Bai mutiny</a> of February 1930, organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viet-nam-quoc-dan-dang">Vietnamese Nationalist Party</a>, and the Nghe-Tinh soviets of 1930–1931, which were suppressed with aerial bombing and mass arrests. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina">Indochina</a> was poorly defended against a major power. After France’s defeat in 1940, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a> forced the governor-general to admit its troops into the north in September 1940 and the south in 1941, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thailand">Thailand</a> attacked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#franco-thai-war">Franco-Thai War</a> of 1940–1941; the French navy won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ko-chang">Ko Chang</a> in January 1941, but Japanese mediation gave Thailand parts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-indochina-war">the First Indochina War</a> from 1946, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-union">French Union</a> force of up to several hundred thousand men, including Vietnamese, Laotian and Cambodian troops, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, losing the frontier posts in 1950 and suffering final defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in May 1954.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#albert-sarraut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-sarraut.jpg" alt="Albert Sarraut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#albert-sarraut">Albert Sarraut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agence_Rol_-_Albert_Sarraut.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agence Rol, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The governor-general, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> and based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanoi">Hanoi</a> from 1902, controlled the federal budget, customs, public works and the army, and ruled with a Superior Council in which colonial officials and business interests were represented. Each of the five territories had its own regime. Cochinchina was a colony governed directly under French law, with a colonial council and a deputy in the French parliament. Annam and Tonkin were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">protectorates</a> where the Nguyen emperor kept his court at Hue and a hierarchy of mandarins, but French residents-superior and provincial residents held real power, and from 1897 the protectorates’ administration was effectively French. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a> remained monarchies under the kings at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#luang-prabang">Luang Phrabang</a>, supervised by French residents, while much of Laos was administered directly. French officials were relatively numerous by colonial standards, and indigenous officials staffed the lower levels. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#albert-sarraut">Albert Sarraut</a>, governor-general in 1911–1914 and 1917–1919, promised ‘Franco-Annamite collaboration’, expanded schooling and reorganised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina-university">University of Hanoi</a>, first founded in 1906, but political rights remained very limited and press and party activity was closely controlled. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-decoux">Admiral Jean Decoux</a> governed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> from 1940 to 1945, promoting youth and sports movements and local patriotism loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philippe-petain">Marshal Pétain</a> while sharing the colony with Japanese forces.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battambang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battambang.jpg" alt="Battambang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battambang">Battambang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Battambang_city.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maharaja1994, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina">French Indochina</a> drew the modern borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a>. French treaties with Siam fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mekong-river">Mekong</a> frontier and returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battambang">Battambang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siem-reap">Siem Reap</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sisophon">Sisophon</a> to Cambodia in 1907, while the boundaries between the five territories became the international borders of three states. The colony transformed the region’s infrastructure: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#haiphong">Haiphong</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kunming">Kunming</a> railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yunnan">Yunnan</a> was completed in 1910, the Long Bien bridge across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-river-asia">Red River</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanoi">Hanoi</a> in 1902, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trans-indochinese-railway">Trans-Indochinese Railway</a> from Hanoi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> in 1936. Saigon, Hanoi and the hill station of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#da-lat">Dalat</a> acquired French boulevards, cathedrals, opera houses and villas that still mark their townscapes. The École française d’Extrême-Orient, founded in 1900, led the study and restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#angkor">Angkor</a> and the scholarship of the region’s languages and religions. The romanised <em>quoc ngu</em> script, promoted by the colonial schools, became the national writing system of Vietnam. Most important, colonial rule produced the movements that ended it: reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phan-boi-chau">Phan Boi Chau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phan-chau-trinh">Phan Chau Trinh</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viet-nam-quoc-dan-dang">Vietnamese Nationalist Party</a> and above all the communist movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh">Nguyen Ai Quoc</a> (Ho Chi Minh), who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochinese-communist-party">Vietnamese Communist Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in February 1930, renamed the Indochinese Communist Party later that year. The wars fought to dissolve the federation shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cold-war">Cold War</a> for decades.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dien-bien-phu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu.jpg" alt="Battle of Dien Bien Phu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victory_in_Battle_of_Dien_Bien_Phu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vietnam People's Army, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> in June 1940 left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina">Indochina</a> isolated, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a>, which wanted to cut supply routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> and gain access to the region’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rubber">rubber</a>, stationed troops there while leaving the French administration in place. On 9 March 1945 the Japanese overthrew French rule in a coup, interning French troops and officials, and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bao-dai">Bao Dai</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sisavang-vong">Sisavang Vong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a> to declare independence. A famine in northern Vietnam in 1944–1945 killed perhaps a million people or more. After Japan’s surrender, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#august-revolution">August Revolution</a>, and on 2 September 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hanoi">Hanoi</a>. France returned in force, with British help in the south, and in 1946 recast the colony as the Indochinese Federation within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-union">French Union</a>. Negotiations failed, and after the French bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#haiphong">Haiphong</a> in November 1946 and the Viet Minh attack in Hanoi on 19 December, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-indochina-war">the First Indochina War</a> began. France granted limited independence to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#state-of-vietnam">Associated States of Vietnam</a> under Bao Dai, Laos and Cambodia in 1949, and Cambodia obtained full independence in 1953. Chinese aid to the Viet Minh from 1950 shifted the balance, and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> on 7 May 1954 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#geneva-accords-1954">Geneva Accords</a> of July 1954, which ended French Indochina and divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indochina">French Indochina</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cambodia">Cambodia</a> in one colonial federation for nearly seventy years, building railways, plantations and export <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a> economies while fixing the region’s borders, and its exploitation and repression gave rise to the nationalist and communist movements whose wars ended French rule at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954.</em></p>

